{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Remove the foreign gods that are among you.</b> It should have said 'that are in your hands,' as it is written: 'And they gave to Jacob all the foreign gods that were in their hands.' For every deed is preceded by thought, and the contemplation of transgression comes before the deed is performed. Therefore he commanded them first to purify the sin of contemplation and said, 'Remove the foreign gods that are among you' — literally. For Jacob said, 'Perhaps my sons have sinned and blessed Elohim in their hearts' (Job 1:5), and they were contemplating the idolatry that was in their hands — 'and purify yourselves.' For through this HaShem will create for you a pure heart. Or one may say: it refers to any contemplation of sin that comes from the side of the evil inclination, which is called 'a foreign god that is among you,' as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 105a): 'There shall be no foreign god in you' (Psalms 81:10) — what foreign god is there within a person's body? You must say: this is the evil inclination. And afterward he warned them about the rectification of the sin of deed, saying: remove the soiled garments and clothe yourselves in fresh garments, for we find in every place that clothing is a designation for deeds, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 9:8): 'At all times let your garments be white.' Of this he said, 'And change your garments.' 'And let us arise and go up to Bethel' — for one may not come to the gate of the King in garments sullied with sin and iniquity, until they first rectify the sin of contemplation and of deed. 'And they gave to Jacob all the foreign gods that were in their hands' — for through this they rectified the sin of deed and of contemplation together: the deed, according to its plain sense, in the nullification of the idolatry that was in their hands; and the removal of the evil inclination, the foreign god that was among them — literally — is also hinted at in their saying 'the foreign gods that were in their hands,' as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 34:10): the wicked are in the domain of their heart, but the righteous — their heart is in their domain, in their hands. And this is 'the foreign gods that were in their hands' — they indicated that that which is 'among you' — behold, it is 'in your hands.' This is a correct allusion, close to hearing."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:02.915769522Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}